I review the list of requested information. Generally, it’s fine to disclose it to the authority as part of an investigation, but I’d advise you to conceal any unnecessary personal data when disclosing the documents, in particular, these ones:

* The number of visitors per month on the BOLT platform (website and mobile application) for the year 2023;
** make sure it’s only the number with no names/email addresses/phone numbers or other identifiers 
* Step-by-step screenshots of the journey of booking a taxi ride on the BOLT website and mobile application;
** I suggest disclosing one from a test/non-live environment to avoid showing any names of real users or their concealed payment details
* Three examples of receipts given to consumers at the end of a taxi trip booked from the BOLT website or application;
** I suggest concealing unnecessary information, such as the pick up/drop off location
* 6 files for handling disputes following a VTC or Taxi ride booked on the BOLT mobile application; 
** I suggest concealing unnecessary information, such as sensitive data, email address, etc.
* A spreadsheet (usable) listing the last 100 taxi journeys carried out by BOLT indicating for each of them, the price range indicated to the customer and the price actually paid by the latter at the end of the journey;
** I suggest adding no names/email addresses/phone numbers or other identifiers, if possible
* A spreadsheet (usable) listing the last 100 VTC races carried out by BOLT indicating for each of them, the price indicated to the customer and the price actually paid by the latter at the end of the race;
** I suggest adding no names/email addresses/phone numbers or other identifiers, if possible

Generally, it is not forbidden to provide the information to the authority as part of an investigation, but I suggest adding effort to conceal data that is not absolutely necessary for them.

